--TEST--
`Core\Http\Stream`'s body is read one way and once: `lines()` strips a trailing `\r` and needs no terminator on the last line, `chunks()` frames nothing, and whichever of the four ran first, the next one throws naming it and leaves no file behind — `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// No socket is opened and no name resolved: the answers come from the table
// (`rule:testing/an-outbound-call-is-answered-from-a-table`).
// A field the origin sent twice, so that both readings of the head are asked
// of the same reply: `header` joins the two lines and `headers` keeps them.
array<string> $trace = ["one", "two"];
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/rows", 200, {body: "alpha\nbeta\r\ngamma", headers: ["x-trace" => $trace]});
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/blob", 200, {body: "raw octets"});
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/events", 200, {body: "event: done\ndata: finished\nid: 9\n\n"});

// A line ends at `\n`, a `\r` before that one is the other half of a CRLF and
// is stripped, and the last line needs no terminator at all — three questions
// one body answers, where a reader that got any of them wrong still prints a
// plausible first line.
var $rows = Core\Http\Client::stream(Core\Http\Method::Get, "https://api.example.com/rows");
echo $rows->status(), " ", $rows->header("x-trace") ?? "no trace", " ", Core\Arr::count($rows->headers("x-trace")), "\n";
foreach ($rows->lines() as tainted string $line) {
    echo "[", $line, "] ", Core\Str::length($line), "\n";
}

// The body is gone rather than merely closed to a second reader, which is what
// makes the refusal a property of the memory as well as of the rule.
try {
    foreach ($rows->chunks() as tainted bytes $late) {
        echo "a second reader walked the body\n";
    }
} catch (LogicError $twice) {
    echo $twice->message, "\n";
}

// `chunks()` frames nothing: what a walk yields is what the transport had, and
// a reply the table answered is one piece of it.
var $blob = Core\Http\Client::stream(Core\Http\Method::Get, "https://api.example.com/blob");
int $pieces = 0;
uint $octets = 0;
foreach ($blob->chunks() as tainted bytes $chunk) {
    $pieces = $pieces + 1;
    $octets = $octets + Core\Bytes::length($chunk);
}
echo "pieces: ", $pieces, " octets: ", $octets, "\n";

// The refusal reaches `saveTo` before the destination is created, so a program
// that was right to be refused is not left holding an empty file at a name it
// chose — the `exists` is the only thing that can tell that apart from the
// throw alone.
try {
    $blob->saveTo("late.bin", 1024);
    echo "a second reader wrote the body to disk\n";
} catch (LogicError $saved) {
    echo $saved->message, "\n";
    if (Core\IO::exists("late.bin")) {
        echo "refused, and an empty file was left behind\n";
    } else {
        echo "refused, and nothing was left behind\n";
    }
}

// And the same rule from the fourth door, so that a reader which learned it for
// one framing still fails here.
var $stream = Core\Http\Client::stream(Core\Http\Method::Get, "https://api.example.com/events");
foreach ($stream->events() as Core\Http\Event $event) {
    echo $event->name() ?? "message", " id=", $event->id() ?? "none", " [", $event->data(), "]\n";
}
try {
    foreach ($stream->events() as Core\Http\Event $again) {
        echo "a second walk found an event\n";
    }
} catch (LogicError $reread) {
    echo $reread->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
200 one, two 2
[alpha] 5
[beta] 4
[gamma] 5
Core\Http\Stream::chunks(): this stream's body was read by `lines()` — a streamed body is read one way and once, and a second reader is refused rather than answered empty, which would be the same answer as a body that was empty
pieces: 1 octets: 10
Core\Http\Stream::saveTo(): this stream's body was read by `chunks()` — a streamed body is read one way and once, and a second reader is refused rather than answered empty, which would be the same answer as a body that was empty
refused, and nothing was left behind
done id=9 [finished]
Core\Http\Stream::events(): this stream's body was read by `events()` — a streamed body is read one way and once, and a second reader is refused rather than answered empty, which would be the same answer as a body that was empty
